"In the first verse of Surah al-Inshiqaq, it is stated: \"When the sky has split open.\" The word \"inshiqaq\" mentioned here means the cracking of the sky by divine command and ultimately its being rolled up. Initially, there is a splitting with clouds, which points to the verse in Surah al-Furqan: \"The day when the sky will split open with clouds.\" This splitting occurs through the terror of the Day of Resurrection and is accompanied by events such as the scattering of the stars when the sky cracks. According to a narration from Ali, this splitting will begin from the region of \"majarrah\"; majarrah has been interpreted as the gate of heaven. The splitting consists of several stages: the initial cracking, followed by splitting with clouds, then the sky melting and becoming like a rose, and finally being rolled up. Thus, this verse encompasses all these stages together.\n\nIn the second verse, the expression \"When it has listened to its Lord\" occurs. This shows that the sky accepted the command to split open submissively and willingly. Just as the sky said \"We come willingly\" during its initial creation, it obeys now as well. The statement \"and it was made fit for it\" conveys that this obedience is what befits the nature of the sky. According to another interpretation, Allah made obedience obligatory upon the sky. That is to say, the sky is destined to split open due to the terror of the Day of Resurrection. These verses are of a complementary nature to the event of the sky splitting. Furthermore, the expression \"haqqat\" (it was fulfilled) means the realization of the Day of Resurrection or the prevailing of truth.\n\nIn the third and fourth verses, it is stated that the earth is stretched and expanded, casts out what is within it, and becomes completely empty. The earth is leveled completely, and the mountains are destroyed. The expression \"casts out what is within it\" means that it casts forth the dead and also pours out the heavy burdens (treasures and minerals) within it. While some scholars intend by this only the bringing forth of the dead, Qatadah accepted both meanings. As for the emptying of the earth, as stated in a hadith according to al-Alusi's narration, it is the earth's casting out what is within it and returning to the state it was in when there was nothing.\n\nIn the fifth verse, it is stated once again: \"When it has listened to its Lord and was made fit for it.\" This indicates the two stages of the events in the heavens and the earth. The words \"idha\" here have been interpreted as conditional or temporal particles. Some stated that the apodosis is omitted, while others stated that the answer is found in the verse: \"At that time everyone will know what they have done\" or \"you will meet your Lord.\" In the sixth verse, humanity is addressed: \"O human being! Indeed, you are laboring toward your Lord with great exertion.\" The verb \"kadh\" means to scratch and to exert serious effort. Man strives toward his Lord through death and what follows, and ultimately meets Him. According to some commentators, this verse is considered the answer to the preceding conditional clauses.\n\nIn the seventh and eighth verses, it is stated that those who are given their record in their right hand will be subjected to an easy reckoning. \"An easy reckoning\" (hisab yasir) means glancing over the record without an interrogation and passing through. In a hadith narrated from Aisha, the Prophet (peace be upon him) stated that this reckoning is merely a presentation (ard) and that a contested reckoning will lead to ruin. In the ninth verse, it is described that this person will return to his people (his family, friends, and the houris in Paradise) in joy. In the tenth verse, as for the one who is given his record behind his back, it is stated that he will cry out, \"Come, O destruction!\" This expression indicates that they will enter Hell. In the eleventh verse, the cause of this punishment is explained: while in the world they were joyful among their people, they assumed they would never return to the Hereafter. The word \"hawr\" expresses returning to decrease after completeness; they thought they would never die.\n\nIn the twelfth to fifteenth verses, it is emphasized that this assumption of the disbelievers is false. In the section beginning with \"Nay,\" it is conveyed that they will return to their Lord and that everything they did was observed. Then Allah swears by the twilight, by the night, and by the moon when it is gathered full. These oaths remind us of the continuous change in the universe and that human beings are also subject to this change. In the nineteenth verse, it is stated: \"You shall surely embark from stage to stage.\" The expression \"tabaqan 'an tabaq\" means passing from state to state, generation to generation, or degree to degree. This reinforces the truth that life in this world is not static and that one will pass into the Hereafter through death. The same verse has also been recited addressing the Prophet, \"you shall surely embark,\" and has been considered an allusion to the Night of the Ascension (Mi'raj). Both recitations carry the promise of elevation for the believers and a descent toward punishment for the disbelievers.\n\nFrom the twentieth verse onward, it is questioned why the deniers do not believe in the face of these truths. In the twenty-first verse, it is stated that when the Quran is recited to them, they do not prostrate. The occasion of revelation for this verse is that the polytheists clapped their hands and whistled when the Prophet prostrated. Abu Hanifah (al-Imam al-A'zam) stated that the prostration of recitation (tilawah) is obligatory in this verse, whereas al-Shafi'i stated that it is Sunnah. In the twenty-second and twenty-third verses, it is expressed that the deniers reject the Quran and that Allah knows what they conceal within themselves. The verb \"yu'un\" means storing and hiding hidden thoughts within a vessel in their hearts. In the twenty-fourth verse, he is commanded to give the disbelievers tidings of a painful punishment. In the twenty-fifth verse, it is stated that those who believe and perform righteous deeds are exempted from this punishment and that for them is an uninterrupted reward. This exception generally represents the separation of the believers from the disbelievers, and it also includes those among the disbelievers who come to faith."